1
00:00:00.238 --> 00:00:04.639
By now, you already know a lot about agents, you understand the agent loop, and how to

2
00:00:04.639 --> 00:00:07.960
make them tick, and how to build something that is useful.

3
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.399
But making something easy to build is just part of that.

4
00:00:11.399 --> 00:00:15.439
The other part is building something that you trust, something that gives you reliable

5
00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:17.319
and repeatable outputs.

6
00:00:17.319 --> 00:00:20.639
So in this video, we're going to talk about guardrails.

7
00:00:20.639 --> 00:00:24.840
Guardrails are a cornerstone on how you can make these agents behave in a way that is

8
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.159
more predictable.

9
00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:30.799
Remember, building agentic systems is so different than regular engineering.

10
00:00:30.799 --> 00:00:34.720
Because in one, you're talking about a very strong type of software, and in the other,

11
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.560
you're talking about a non-deterministic software.

12
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:42.159
So guardrails play a huge role in here on how you can enforce these agents to behave

13
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:43.159
in different ways.

14
00:00:43.159 --> 00:00:44.759
This is going to be very exciting.

15
00:00:44.759 --> 00:00:46.479
Let's go into it.

16
00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:50.919
Guardrails will allow you to add more deterministic control in your agents, and it's going to

17
00:00:50.919 --> 00:00:52.720
allow you to do it in two ways.

18
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.040
You're going to use LLMs as a judge, or you're going to use deterministic code.

19
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.400
And we're going to talk about both, and how they play a role in our ReAct loop.

20
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.000
The next module, we're also going to talk about create-flows, which is basically the

21
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.760
outmost control if you want to go all the way into that.

22
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.959
But guardrails might just be able to solve most of our problems when you're trying to

23
00:01:14.959 --> 00:01:18.279
enforce specific output guidelines.

24
00:01:18.279 --> 00:01:22.400
We already have talked about this ReAct loop, which starts with the tasks and eventually

25
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.239
produces a series of actions and observations that lead to an answer.

26
00:01:26.239 --> 00:01:30.000
And there is nothing deterministic about this process, and that is the problem.

27
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.599
For most use cases, they need very specific answers.

28
00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:38.360
It's not necessarily that you want the same answer every time, but rather that you want

29
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.839
a reliable, repeatable outcome.

30
00:01:40.839 --> 00:01:46.000
So the answer can be different, but it needs to be satisfactory every time you execute

31
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.000
it.

32
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.319
Now, we're already talking about this idea of a conversation and how your features and

33
00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:54.559
prompting impact the systems.

34
00:01:54.559 --> 00:01:58.279
And if you really think about it, these models are all about the masses and the prompts that

35
00:01:58.279 --> 00:01:59.839
you're sending into them.

36
00:01:59.839 --> 00:02:05.639
And it's in this mix of this agent's inner thought process that hallucinations might

37
00:02:05.639 --> 00:02:06.639
happen.

38
00:02:06.639 --> 00:02:10.320
And the models might come up with answers that are not necessarily correct, or they

39
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.320
start deviating a little bit from what you actually want, not only in terms of the content,

40
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.440
but also in terms of the format, or in terms of the style.

41
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:25.600
And that's where Guardrails can fit right in as a safe check, as a final guard to make

42
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.559
sure that the answer is satisfactory.

43
00:02:28.559 --> 00:02:33.919
And later in Module 3, we're also going to talk about CrewAI Flows that I'm very excited

44
00:02:33.919 --> 00:02:40.679
because Flows is the outmost way for you to build these control layers into your automations.

45
00:02:40.679 --> 00:02:46.000
But Guardrails are amazing because they allow you to work with a lot of agency and still

46
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.160
enforce a specific output.

47
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.960
The idea here is that you can use a Guardrail to validate the actual answer, and then one

48
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.119
of two things can happen.

49
00:02:55.119 --> 00:02:58.000
First, it doesn't pass that Guardrail.

50
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.479
So the answer doesn't meet whatever criteria you define it should do.

51
00:03:02.479 --> 00:03:05.320
And it then goes back into the loop.

52
00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:11.000
But the Guardrail can actually provide extra feedback on why the answer is wrong, as it's

53
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.440
injecting it back into the ReAct loop, so the agent can do better.

54
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.440
And then if the agent try another step at that, but now it passes, then the Guardrail

55
00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:25.119
allows to go ahead in the next step and give you the actual answer.

56
00:03:25.119 --> 00:03:29.820
So the cool thing about Guardrails is that they allow you to validate information midway

57
00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:33.600
through a process because they operate at the task level.

58
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.839
And not only that, they also let you inject feedback back into the loop.

59
00:03:37.839 --> 00:03:43.360
You can think this is a more deterministic human in the loop, where you also can have

60
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.000
not only your agents talk to a human in the loop for real, but now programmatically check

61
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.000
that output.

62
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.080
There are two kinds of Guardrails that you can use.

63
00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.880
One is an LLM Guardrail, what is basically using another LLM as a judge, where you have

64
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:08.479
this LLM fact check or do something with that message in order to see if it's good or not.

65
00:04:08.479 --> 00:04:14.119
And the other one is a Code Guardrail, where you can basically write actual code that will

66
00:04:14.119 --> 00:04:17.920
validate to see if the answer is right or wrong.

67
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.880
And you can do both of those on CrewAI out of the box.

68
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.559
The LLM Guardrail is a little more loose because it depends on another LLM, but it's extremely

69
00:04:26.559 --> 00:04:28.079
easy to build.

70
00:04:28.079 --> 00:04:32.920
While the traditional Code Guardrail requires you to do some extra coding, but allows you

71
00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.399
to be as specific as you want.

72
00:04:35.399 --> 00:04:40.040
So if you think about the LLM Guardrail, what happens here is, as the agent gets the

73
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.880
final answer, it goes into this LLM Guardrail.

74
00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.799
And then the Guardrail calls a separate LLM, checking if this answer is reasonable, given

75
00:04:50.799 --> 00:04:52.399
some certain criteria.

76
00:04:52.399 --> 00:04:55.880
And if it's not, then it goes back into the thought process.

77
00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.940
And if it's good, it can move on.

78
00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:03.959
An example here for the Code Guardrail would actually be something a little more deterministic,

79
00:05:03.959 --> 00:05:07.279
like checking, for example, the answer length.

80
00:05:07.279 --> 00:05:10.600
So you can check what is the answer and count the characters.

81
00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:15.519
And if the characters are less than 200, because you have a hard requirement that needs to

82
00:05:15.519 --> 00:05:20.799
be a short answer, then the code is good to go and the task is done.

83
00:05:20.799 --> 00:05:24.359
But if it's not, it then sends it back to the ReAct loop.

84
00:05:24.359 --> 00:05:29.299
So in here, you can see that with the Code Guardrails, you can be very specific and use

85
00:05:29.299 --> 00:05:32.480
regular Python to implement these checks.

86
00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:38.079
And with LLMs, you can just tell another LLM to fact check something or to do any sort

87
00:05:38.079 --> 00:05:41.000
of other checks that the LLM might be able to.

88
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.440
The cool thing about this, it doesn't matter if you're using a Code Guardrail or if you're

89
00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.040
using an LLM Guardrail, there is always some feedback on the reason why it didn't pass

90
00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.160
that Guardrail that you can send back to the ReAct loop.

91
00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:00.440
In the case of an LLM Guardrail, that will be automatically generated and send it back.

92
00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:06.000
But in the case of a Code Guardrail, you can dynamically set what is the reason, telling

93
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.399
maybe this answer is too long, it needs to be only 200 characters long.

94
00:06:10.399 --> 00:06:15.359
And that will send that information back into ReAct loop and allow your agent to take in

95
00:06:15.359 --> 00:06:21.079
that information and try to rewrite that final answer or maybe take more actions in order

96
00:06:21.079 --> 00:06:24.959
to achieve something that is acceptable by the Guardrail.

97
00:06:24.959 --> 00:06:28.679
Let me show you some real quick code to show you how this works.

98
00:06:28.679 --> 00:06:34.799
For a Code Guardrail, the way that you would do is just define a simple function in Python.

99
00:06:34.799 --> 00:06:40.640
And that function will take an output as a string that will be basically its argument.

100
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.119
It's going to be what the agent is sending to this function to be validated.

101
00:06:45.119 --> 00:06:48.119
Inside that function, you can do anything that you want.

102
00:06:48.119 --> 00:06:52.320
In this case, we're keeping it simple and just checking if the word count is less than

103
00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.359
200.

104
00:06:53.359 --> 00:06:58.760
If that is true, that will allow your agent to progress on and give its final answer and

105
00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.839
move on with the next one.

106
00:07:00.839 --> 00:07:07.200
But if that is false, you can also include a reason in here on why it's not good.

107
00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.160
And that information is going to be injected back into the agent to try it once more.

108
00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.880
And in order for you to actually add that Guardrail to the agent itself, all you got

109
00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.279
to do is add this new attribute to your task that is Guardrail.

110
00:07:22.279 --> 00:07:26.279
And you can add multiple as well so that it takes an array as a value, meaning that

111
00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:28.959
you can add many different Guardrails.

112
00:07:28.959 --> 00:07:35.420
Not only a Code Guardrail, but an LLM Guardrail as well, or however many you want to be there.

113
00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:40.600
Another way that you also want to think about getting better outcomes is by enforcing structured

114
00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:41.600
outputs.

115
00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:46.059
And that just allows you to make sure that whatever you're getting out of your agents

116
00:07:46.059 --> 00:07:50.440
and crew, it's following a specific format that you expect.

117
00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:54.200
And this can be super helpful, especially if you're planning to do something with that

118
00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:55.200
data.

119
00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.359
So if you're sending this data somewhere, like an external application, or if you're

120
00:07:59.359 --> 00:08:04.399
trying to take an action based on this output, it might be better for you to understand what

121
00:08:04.399 --> 00:08:07.399
exactly is the format that you're getting.

122
00:08:07.399 --> 00:08:12.880
And you can use Pydantic here to actually enforce this format and making sure that the

123
00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.839
output of the crew always map to what you need.

124
00:08:16.839 --> 00:08:21.519
So one of the options that you have to enforce the particular format is to use a dedicated

125
00:08:21.519 --> 00:08:22.519
formatter agent.

126
00:08:22.519 --> 00:08:28.279
And this strategy is easier to test because it makes the formatting a specialized role.

127
00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:33.320
The ultimate formatting enforcement is using Pydantic models though, where you can actually

128
00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:39.760
use a separate LLM if you want to, just to do that structured function calling output.

129
00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:46.020
So you can create your own class, for example, this user query class that uses Pydantic base

130
00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:50.460
model to define a series of attributes for that class.

131
00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:55.659
Then now you can actually pass that class as the example of the output that you want

132
00:08:55.659 --> 00:08:57.739
to get from this agent.

133
00:08:57.739 --> 00:09:03.820
And the agent will actually enforce to get that output out of the LLM, either by using

134
00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:09.820
function calling, if that LLM has that available, or by asking it to return JSON in that specific

135
00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:12.539
format and checking against that.

136
00:09:12.539 --> 00:09:17.820
And the cool thing here is that you may choose to get either a Pydantic object instance at

137
00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.979
the end of this, or the JSON structure for this object.

138
00:09:21.979 --> 00:09:26.460
And you can specify that by setting either output Pydantic or output JSON.

139
00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:32.419
And in that way, you make sure that you always get the same format output that you care about.

140
00:09:32.419 --> 00:09:36.059
And then you can do whatever you want with that structured output.

141
00:09:36.059 --> 00:09:42.059
So for example, in this user query example, you might want to push the text into a database,

142
00:09:42.059 --> 00:09:48.340
but only if the confidence score is above five or seven or whatever it might be.

143
00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:53.260
So you can see that by defining not only the object, but the types of the attributes that

144
00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:58.539
you're getting, allows you now to use these attributes to do conditional logic, or many

145
00:09:58.539 --> 00:10:00.179
other things.

146
00:10:00.179 --> 00:10:05.020
For now, I'm very excited about the next topic, and that is execution hooks.

147
00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:10.500
We very high level mentioning a few lessons ago, but now we're going to go deep into why

148
00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.460
they exist and how you can use them.

149
00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:17.460
Especially now that we're bringing all those things together, what is the reason why you

150
00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:22.140
have hooks, and what are some of the interesting things that it will allow you to do for your

151
00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:23.340
use cases.

